odules and capabilities it actually supports.\n\n## Proposed contract\n\n### `core.*` — mandatory protocol kernel\n\nShared by every implementation:\n\n- Protocol and module-version negotiation.\n- Authenticated principal and connection scopes.\n- Node identity, role, and capabilities.\n- Request IDs, deadlines, cancellation, and structured errors.\n- Pagination and subscription conventions.\n- Health and connection-generation information.\n\nIllustrative handshake:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"method\": \"core.hello\",\n  \"params\": {\n    \"protocol_version\": \"1\",\n    \"supported_modules\": {\n      \"realm\": [\"1\"],\n      \"execution\": [\"1\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nResponse:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"node_id\": \"node_...\",\n  \"roles\": [\"personal\", \"executor\"],\n  \"modules\": {\n    \"realm\": \"1\",\n    \"execution\": \"1\",\n    \"sync\": \"1\",\n    \"admin\": \"1\"\n  },\n  \"capabilities\": {\n    \"workspace\": true,\n    \"shell\": true,\n    \"local_inference\": false\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIdentity must come from the authenticated channel. Methods do not accept a caller-selected `user_id`. A hosted implementation maps the session to its tenant and RLS scope; a single-user node maps it to its sole local owner.\n\n### `realm.*` — durable user-facing domain state\n\nOwns portable personal state:\n\n- Chats, branches, messages, and finalized Runs.\n- Knowledge Space catalogue and records.\n- Agent Profiles, memories, and user settings.\n- Mounts and Authority Connections.\n- Durable placement records and recovery preferences.\n- Deletion and tombstone operations.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nrealm.chats.create\nrealm.messages.append\nrealm.chats.fork\nrealm.knowledge.list\nrealm.knowledge.read\nrealm.settings.update\n```\n\nPersonal Realm operations may work offline and synchronize later. Their write contract therefore needs stable IDs, causal parents or expected revisions, and explicit conflict/fork results.\n\nIt exposes domain operations—not SQL, table rows, or gen